On Tuesday 25 November 2003 13:19, Robin Lee Powell wrote:
> I actually would expect elements to be fu'ivla; they seem to be an
> obvious place for them.  Or possibly lujvo based on atomic weight.

Many of them are (e.g. {sodnrkali} "potassium"). The navni (except for navni 
itself) are all calques: solnavni, navni, laznynavni, mipnavni, ciznavni, 
dircynavni. The kliru are calques except for kliru itself and klirlxastati 
(oops - that should have been {klirnxastati}).

In English too, many of the names are fu'ivla: sulfur, arsenic, palladium. A 
few are native: iron, gold, tin. Some are from other Germanic languages: 
bismuth, wolfram/tungsten, nickel. The ones named for people and places, such 
as rutherfordium, you can pretty much count on being fu'ivla in every 
language (though for francium, gallium, and germanium I made fu'ivla).

> But if one was to make a lujvo that semantically represented an
> element, I wouldn't have any expectation whatsoever of understanding
> it, because my chemistry isn't good enough.  While I don't always
> like they way Pierre creates words, I do trust his domain knowledge
> enough to expect that 'light type-of chlorine' would not be
> incomprehensible to a chemist as flourine, and that silna has at
> least something to do with ionization, conceptually.

I didn't make up {li'orkliru}. It's in oldlog.txt, which is a list of Loglan 
gismu ({fluro}, in this case) with corresponding Lojban words.

"Salt" is used in chemistry for a large class of ionic compounds, not just 
sodium chloride or sea salt (which is a mixture of salts). I also suggested a 
while back using {-tau} for binary compounds, not necessarily salts, such as 
tabno relkijytau (va'i bakrygapci, because it results from dropping acid on 
chalk) and tabno pavykijytau (va'i mantygapci, because it's the anhydride of 
mantyslami).

